Developing phpz extensions requires a Zig toolchain and a PHP development environment. These docs use Zig **0.17.0** and support PHP **8.2–8.5**. For Windows packages and build steps, switch to 简体中文 and open Windows in the sidebar.

# [Zig toolchain]($heading.id('zig-toolchain'))

Download the toolchain for your system and architecture from the [Zig website](https://ziglang.org/download/) and add the extracted directory to `PATH`. See the [Zig getting started guide](https://ziglang.org/learn/getting-started/) for installation instructions.

```sh
zig version
```

# [PHP development environment]($heading.id('php-development-environment'))

On Linux / macOS, you need:

- PHP CLI to run the skeleton generator, Stub generator, and tests.
- PHP development headers for Zig to translate the PHP C API.
- `php-config` to locate headers and identify the PHP version.
- The PHP tokenizer extension for `gen_stub.php` to parse Stubs.

Use the CLI and headers from the same PHP installation, matching the version, architecture, NTS/ZTS mode, and Debug configuration. Packages for running PHP websites alone may not include development headers.

## [Install using a package manager]($heading.id('install-using-a-package-manager'))

Choose the commands for your system and check that your package source provides PHP 8.2–8.5.

Debian / Ubuntu:

```sh
sudo apt update
sudo apt install php-cli php-dev curl
```

macOS (Homebrew):

```sh
brew install php
```

See the [PHP Debian installation guide](https://www.php.net/manual/en/install.unix.debian.php) and the [Homebrew PHP formula](https://formulae.brew.sh/formula/php) for package details. Continue with the environment checks below after installation.

## [Build Debug PHP from source]($heading.id('build-php-from-source'))

A separate PHP installation configured with `--enable-debug` helps investigate extension memory and lifecycle issues. This Linux example builds PHP 8.5 into a user directory, separate from the system PHP.

Build dependencies on Debian / Ubuntu:

```sh
sudo apt install build-essential autoconf bison re2c pkg-config git curl libxml2-dev libsqlite3-dev
```

For other systems, see the [PHP source build instructions](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/PHP-8.5/README.md#building-php-source-code). Run these commands in a working directory:

```sh
git clone --depth 1 --branch PHP-8.5 https://github.com/php/php-src.git php-src-8.5
cd php-src-8.5
./buildconf
phpz_php_prefix="$HOME/phpz-dev/php-8.5-debug"
./configure --enable-debug \
  --prefix="$phpz_php_prefix" \
  --with-config-file-path="$phpz_php_prefix/etc"
make -j4
make install
```

`PHP-8.5` is a maintenance branch; pin a tag or commit when you need a reproducible environment. `-j4` runs four compilation jobs; adjust it for your machine. To test ZTS, add `--enable-zts` during configuration and use a separate installation directory.

Create a development `php.ini` that displays runtime and startup errors:

```sh
mkdir -p "$phpz_php_prefix/etc"
cat > "$phpz_php_prefix/etc/php.ini" <<'INI'
error_reporting = E_ALL
display_errors = On
display_startup_errors = On
log_errors = On
INI
export PATH="$phpz_php_prefix/bin:$PATH"
```

This `PATH` change applies to the current terminal. Select this PHP installation again in a new terminal, and use `php --ini` to check which configuration file it loads. See also [Zend's PHP build environment guide](https://www.zend.com/resources/php-extensions/setting-up-your-php-build-environment).

# [Check the environment]($heading.id('check-the-environment'))

```sh
php -v
php-config --version
php-config --include-dir
php --ini
php -r 'var_export(extension_loaded("tokenizer")); echo PHP_EOL;'
php -i
```

The tokenizer check should print `true`. In `php -i`, verify `Thread Safety`, `Debug Build`, and the extension build information. The source Debug build should report `Debug Build` as `yes`. The directory from `php-config --include-dir` should contain `main`, `Zend`, `TSRM`, and `ext`.

When multiple PHP installations are available, select matching `php` and `php-config` executables. Pass the chosen configuration tool path through `--with-php-config` when generating the skeleton.

# [Next steps]($heading.id('next-steps'))

Continue to [Hello World]($link.page('getting-started/hello-world')) to generate and run `my_php_extension`.

For an existing Zig extension project, you can also add phpz as a dependency:

```sh
zig fetch --save git+https://github.com/happystraw/phpz
```

Keep the URL and hash in `build.zig.zon`. After upgrading dependencies or the toolchain, regenerate arginfo, rebuild, and run the PHPT tests.
